## The least affordable metros are not the most expensive ones
<!-- claim -->
**{{ v('ch5.message') }}**
<!-- step: raw -->
Rank the {{ v('ch5.metros_ranked') }} metropolitan areas by the price of the median home in {{ v('ch5.year') }} and you get the ranking every list publishes. The three most expensive are {{ v('ch5.raw.top3') }}; the three cheapest are {{ v('ch5.raw.bottom3') }}. {{ v('ch5.micropolitan_excluded') }} micropolitan areas are in the warehouse and the explorer but not in this ranking, because the one year census release covers only the largest of them and a ranking with holes in it is not a ranking.
<!-- step: adjusted -->
Now rank the same metros by the payment on that home as a share of what the median household there earns, and carry the census margins of error through {{ v('ch5.draws') }} Monte Carlo draws so that every rank has an interval. The three least affordable are {{ v('ch5.adjusted.top3') }}; the three most affordable are {{ v('ch5.adjusted.bottom3') }}. The least affordable metro, {{ v('ch5.least_affordable') }}, asks {{ v('ch5.least_affordable.payment_share') }} of the median income (interval {{ v('ch5.least_affordable.low') }} to {{ v('ch5.least_affordable.high') }}); the most affordable, {{ v('ch5.most_affordable') }}, asks {{ v('ch5.most_affordable.payment_share') }}. The rank correlation between the two rankings is {{ v('ch5.spearman') }}, the mean rank change is {{ v('ch5.mean_abs_rank_change') }} places and the largest is {{ v('ch5.max_rank_change') }}, for {{ v('ch5.biggest_mover') }}, from {{ v('ch5.biggest_mover.raw_rank') }} by price to {{ v('ch5.biggest_mover.adjusted_rank') }} by payment share. Among the {{ v('ch5.largest') }} largest metros, {{ v('ch5.movers') }} move beyond their rank interval. A third measure, the share of local households whose income clears the payment on the median home at the {{ v('policy.affordable_line') }} line, runs from {{ v('ch5.affordable_share.min') }} to {{ v('ch5.affordable_share.max') }} across the metros with a median of {{ v('ch5.affordable_share.median') }}.
<!-- method -->
Plan `{{ v('ch5.plan_hash') }}`. {{ v('ch5.method.estimator') }}; {{ v('ch5.method.interval') }}; {{ v('ch5.method.split') }}; {{ v('ch5.method.family') }}. On the simulator, where the true affordability ranking is known and the expensive metros were given the high incomes on purpose, the adjusted ranking's rank correlation with the truth is {{ v('recovery.ranking.adjusted') }} against {{ v('recovery.ranking.raw') }} for the price ranking, and the bracket interpolation's mean absolute error against the exact affordable share is {{ v('recovery.bracket_interpolation.mean_bias') }}.
